&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;LICENSE.md&#34; &gt;LICENSE.md&lt;/a&gt; for more information. Contributions to this project
are accepted under the same license.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Individual files contain the following tag instead of the full license text.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;SPDX-License-Identifier:    BSD-3-Clause
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This enables machine processing of license information based on the SPDX
License Identifiers that are here available: &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;http://spdx.org/licenses/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;http://spdx.org/licenses/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;related-projects&#34;&gt;Related projects
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/geky/littlefs-fuse&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs-fuse&lt;/a&gt; - A &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/libfuse/libfuse&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;FUSE&lt;/a&gt; wrapper for littlefs. The project allows you to
mount littlefs directly on a Linux machine. Can be useful for debugging
littlefs if you have an SD card handy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/geky/littlefs-js&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs-js&lt;/a&gt; - A javascript wrapper for littlefs. I&amp;rsquo;m not sure why you would
want this, but it is handy for demos.  You can see it in action
&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;http://littlefs.geky.net/demo.html&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://pypi.org/project/littlefs-python/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs-python&lt;/a&gt; - A Python wrapper for littlefs. The project allows you
to create images of the filesystem on your PC. Check if littlefs will fit
your needs, create images for a later download to the target memory or
inspect the content of a binary image of the target memory.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://crates.io/crates/littlefs2&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs2-rust&lt;/a&gt; - A Rust wrapper for littlefs. This project allows you
to use littlefs in a Rust-friendly API, reaping the benefits of Rust&amp;rsquo;s memory
safety and other guarantees.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/Graveflo/nim-littlefs&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;nim-littlefs&lt;/a&gt; - A Nim wrapper and API for littlefs. Includes a fuse
implementation based on &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/geky/littlefs-fuse&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs-fuse&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/yomimono/chamelon&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;chamelon&lt;/a&gt; - A pure-OCaml implementation of (most of) littlefs, designed for
use with the MirageOS library operating system project. It is interoperable
with the reference implementation, with some caveats.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/tniessen/littlefs-disk-img-viewer&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;littlefs-disk-img-viewer&lt;/a&gt; - A memory-efficient web application for viewing
littlefs disk images in your web browser.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/whitecatboard/Lua-RTOS-ESP32/tree/master/components/mklfs/src&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;mklfs&lt;/a&gt; - A command line tool for creating littlefs images. Used in the Lua
RTOS ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/earlephilhower/mklittlefs&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;mklittlefs&lt;/a&gt; - A command line tool for creating littlefs images. Used in the
ESP8266 and RP2040 ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/oyama/pico-littlefs-usb&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;pico-littlefs-usb&lt;/a&gt; - An interface for littlefs that emulates a FAT12
filesystem over USB. Allows mounting littlefs on a host PC without additional
drivers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/geky/ramcrc32bd&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;ramcrc32bd&lt;/a&gt; - An example block device using littlefs&amp;rsquo;s 32-bit CRC for
error-correction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/geky/ramrsbd&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;ramrsbd&lt;/a&gt; - An example block device using Reed-Solomon codes for
error-correction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/armmbed/mbed-os&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Mbed OS&lt;/a&gt; - The easiest way to get started with littlefs is to jump into Mbed
which already has block device drivers for most forms of embedded storage.
littlefs is available in Mbed OS as the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://os.mbed.com/docs/mbed-os/latest/apis/littlefilesystem.html&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;LittleFileSystem&lt;/a&gt; class.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/pellepl/spiffs&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;SPIFFS&lt;/a&gt; - Another excellent embedded filesystem for NOR flash. As a more
traditional logging filesystem with full static wear-leveling, SPIFFS will
likely outperform littlefs on small memories such as the internal flash on
microcontrollers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://github.com/dlbeer/dhara&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Dhara&lt;/a&gt; - An interesting NAND flash translation layer designed for small
MCUs. It offers static wear-leveling and power-resilience with only a fixed
&lt;em&gt;O(|address|)&lt;/em&gt; pointer structure stored on each block and in RAM.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;http://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/00index_e.html&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;ChaN&amp;rsquo;s FatFs&lt;/a&gt; - A lightweight reimplementation of the infamous FAT filesystem
for microcontroller-scale devices. Due to limitations of FAT it can&amp;rsquo;t provide
power-loss resilience, but it does allow easy interop with PCs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
</description>
